{
 'title': 'How desert animals survive', 'context': 'A lecture in a biology course on how animals cope with heat and lack of water in deserts.',
 'vocab': ['metabolic water', 'nocturnal', 'burrow', 'fog', 'dormant'],
 'script': [
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Good afternoon. Today we're looking at how animals survive in deserts, which are among the most difficult environments on Earth. An animal living in a hot desert faces two connected problems. The first is heat: during the day, the surface of the sand can become hot enough to burn. The second is water: rain may fall only a few times a year, or not at all for several years. And the two problems make each other worse, because the usual way for a mammal to cool down is to sweat or pant, and both of those use water."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "The simplest solution is to avoid the heat altogether. The great majority of small desert mammals are nocturnal, meaning they're active at night and spend the day underground. A burrow just half a metre below the surface can be dramatically cooler than the sand above it, and the air inside is more humid, so the animal loses less water as it breathes."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Some animals have bodies that are designed to get rid of heat. A good example is the fennec fox of the Sahara, the smallest fox in the world. Its ears are enormous in proportion to its body, and they contain many blood vessels close to the surface, so they work like radiators, releasing heat into the air. The fennec also has thick fur on the soles of its feet, which protects it from the burning sand and helps it to walk on loose dunes."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "But the more remarkable adaptations are those that deal with water. Consider the kangaroo rat, which lives in the deserts of North America. In the laboratory, kangaroo rats have been kept for long periods on a diet of dry seeds without drinking at all. How is that possible? Well, when any animal breaks down food to release energy, water is produced as a by-product. This is known as metabolic water. For most animals, it's only a small part of their needs, but the kangaroo rat loses so little water that metabolic water is almost enough. Its kidneys produce extremely concentrated urine, and its nose is cooler than the rest of its body, so when it breathes out, much of the moisture in its breath condenses and is recovered."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Other animals collect water in ingenious ways. In the Namib Desert in south-western Africa, which lies along the coast, it hardly ever rains, but fog regularly drifts in from the Atlantic Ocean in the early morning. Certain beetles climb to the top of a dune and stand with their heads down and their backs raised into the wind. Tiny droplets of water from the fog collect on their backs and then run down towards their mouths. Engineers have since studied these beetles in the hope of designing materials that can collect water from the air."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "In Australia, a small lizard called the thorny devil has a different method. Its skin is covered in tiny channels between the scales. When it stands on damp sand, or when rain or dew touches its body, water is drawn along these channels by capillary action, rather like water moving up a paper towel, until it reaches the corners of its mouth."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Birds face a special challenge, because their chicks can't travel to water. Sandgrouse, which live in the deserts of Africa and Asia, have solved this in an extraordinary way. The male flies to a waterhole, sometimes many kilometres from the nest, and wades in so that his breast feathers soak up water like a sponge. He then flies back, and the chicks drink directly from his feathers."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Finally, some animals simply wait. Spadefoot toads, for instance, spend most of the year buried in the ground in a dormant state, sometimes for many months. When heavy rain finally arrives, they come to the surface, call loudly and breed in temporary pools. Because those pools may dry up within days, their tadpoles develop extremely quickly, in some species turning into young toads in little more than a week. What all of these examples show is that there's no single solution to desert life. In the next lecture, we'll look at desert plants, which face the same problems but cannot move."),
 ],
 'groups': [
  G('Questions 31–40. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.', [
   Q('note', 'Sweating and panting cool the body but use __________', 'water', 'both of those use water', 'Both use water.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Air in a burrow is cooler and more __________ than outside', 'humid', 'the air inside is more humid', 'Burrow air is more humid.', max=1),
   Q('note', "The fennec's ears act like __________ that release heat", 'radiators', 'they work like radiators', 'They work like radiators.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Fur on the fennec\'s __________ protects it from hot sand', 'feet', 'thick fur on the soles of its feet', 'Its feet have fur.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Kangaroo rats were kept on a diet of dry __________', 'seeds', 'on a diet of dry seeds without drinking at all', 'They ate dry seeds.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Water produced when food is broken down is called __________ water', 'metabolic', 'This is known as metabolic water.', 'It is metabolic water.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Namib beetles collect water from __________ coming off the Atlantic', 'fog', 'fog regularly drifts in from the Atlantic Ocean', 'They use fog.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'The thorny devil\'s skin has tiny __________ that carry water to its mouth', 'channels', 'Its skin is covered in tiny channels between the scales.', 'Water moves along channels.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Male sandgrouse carry water to chicks in their breast __________', 'feathers', 'his breast feathers soak up water like a sponge', 'Feathers soak up water.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Spadefoot toads breed in temporary __________ after heavy rain', 'pools', 'breed in temporary pools', 'They breed in pools.', max=1),
  ]),
 ],
},
